1. The function is declared `unsafe` and returns a raw pointer (`json.add(position)`). The global `error` struct now holds `json: Vec<u8>` and `position: usize`, so the pointer arithmetic is unnecessary. The same information can be returned safely as the slice of the buffer starting at `position`.
2. A reference into the mutex-guarded data cannot outlive the lock guard. The function therefore returns an owned `Vec<u8>` copy of the remaining bytes, `json[position..]`. A `&[u8]` would not be possible here.
3. `position` may exceed the length, so it is clamped with `min` to avoid a panic. No `unsafe` is needed.

The unsafe pointer return becomes an owned `Vec<u8>` (the C-string tail from the error position).

